   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend public health law, in relation to the myalgic\nencephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome education and outreach program",
    "basePrintNo": "S6928",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6928-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 27,
       "fullName": "Brad Hoylman-Sigal",
       "imgName": "438_brad_hoylman.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 438,
       "sessionMemberId": 1813,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "HOYLMAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §207, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6928\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend public health law, in relation to the myalgic\nencephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome education and outreach\nprogram\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill adds myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome to the\neducation and outreach program administered by the Department of Health.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends subdivision 1 of section 207 of the public\nhealth law by adding a new paragraph (p) on myalgic\nencephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date of the bill.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation will help ensure that New York state is providing more\neducation and outreach regarding Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic\nFatigue Syndrome (ME/CFS), a multi-system disease associated with neuro-\nlogical, immunological and energy metabolism impairment characterized by\nlong standing and debilitating fatigue with a delayed exacerbation of\nsymptoms and loss of stamina following even minimal mental or physical\nfunction. Symptoms may also include cognitive dysfunction, sleep abnor-\nmalities, autonomic manifestations, and pain. It is estimated ME/CFS\naffects up to one million Americans, but the actual numbers could be\nmuch higher.  According to the Institute of Medicine, an estimated\n84-91% of individuals have not yet been diagnosed with ME/CFS. Women are\nfar more likely to have this illness than men with a ratio of 4 to 1 and\nat least 256 of ME/CFS patients are confined to their home or bed.  The\ncause of this illness remains unknown and there are no diagnostic tools,\nnor an existing cure. There are however therapies available to manage\nsymptoms.\n\nEducation and outreach to the public and health care providers about\nME/CFS is very important, as is the need for more research to discover\nthe causes, understand the mechanisms of the disease and develop effec-\ntive diagnostic markers and treatments. Unfortunately, the term \"Chronic\nFatigue Syndrome\" has a negative connotation and can trivialize the\nseriousness of the condition and promote misunderstanding of the\nillness. This makes the need for education and outreach even more impor-\ntant.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nOn the one hundred eightieth day after it becomes a law.\n",
